// Expected: a0a1 at one session and a1a1 at two.
//
// One constant, two deliveries in two phases. Alice MACs x under a key Bob
// and Carol share with her, hands the pair to Bob in phase 0, leaks the key
// in phase 1 and only then hands the same pair to Carol. Bob's delivery is
// safe: forging the tag for it needs the key in phase 0, and the key is not
// disclosed until phase 1. Carol's delivery is not: when it happens the key
// is public, so the attacker forges a tag over a value of its own and Carol
// accepts it.
//
// The engine used to judge every delivery of a constant against the phase of
// its first, so a forgery derivable only in phase 1 was refused at Carol
// because Bob had been handed the pair in phase 0. An install now carries
// the earliest phase whose knowledge builds it and reaches only the
// recipients delivered from that phase on: Carol sees the forgery, Bob's
// view stays honest. At two sessions each delivery is a replay of the other
// session's pair, since the key is shared across sessions.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private k
	generates x
	t = MAC(k, x)
]

Alice -> Bob: x, t

principal Bob[
	knows private k
	_ = ASSERT(MAC(k, x), t)?
]

phase[1]

principal Alice[
	leaks k
]

Alice -> Carol: x, t

principal Carol[
	knows private k
	_ = ASSERT(MAC(k, x), t)?
]

queries[
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: x
	authentication? Alice -> Carol: x
]
